      title: "When You Need to Advertise in the Worst Possible Way: ten steps to failure, read backward",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 1998)", section: "Chapter 34 · When You Need to Advertise in the Worst Possible Way · p. 78", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Fill your ad with the kinds of phrases and claims you’ve heard in other commercials. Your ad should sound like an ad!", page: "78" }
      ],
      idea: "This one-page chapter is **satire**, and the title is a pun. “Needing to advertise in the worst possible way” sounds like desperate urgency, but Roy means it literally: here's how to advertise as badly as possible. Every 'step' is a belief he has spent the book taking apart. **Flip each one and you have the rules.**\n\n1. **Reach only the 'right people.'** Flip: it's not who you reach, it's what you say (Chapters 14–15).\n2. **Reach as many right people as possible; frequency doesn't matter.** Flip: repetition beats reach (Chapter 27; mistake #2 in Chapter 35).\n3. **Don't worry what the ad says, just make the delivery smooth, professional and classy.** Flip: content first. The public no longer trusts 'smooth' (Chapter 25).\n4. **Fill it with phrases from other commercials. Make it sound like an ad!** Flip: a good ad doesn't sound like an ad (Chapters 25 and 31).\n5. **Kill any ad your friends and neighbors don't like. Anything that makes people uncomfortable can't work.** Flip: powerful ads draw fire, and the line that bothers people is often the strongest one (Chapter 23). Complaints usually arrive with success (Chapter 24).\n6. **Run a TV or radio ad 20–30 times, then change it, since everyone's heard it.** Flip: they've barely started to remember it. Stay with the nail (Chapter 27).\n7. **Don't worry whether the medium is intrusive; passive works just as well.** Flip: intrusive media wins the customer before she's shopping (Chapters 16, 24, 30).\n8. **Assume the public knows your product intimately and use industry language.** Flip: translate every feature into the customer's language (Chapter 43; mistake #3 in Chapter 35).\n9. **Impress the public with your superior taste; assume they're paying close attention.** Flip: the customer is seldom paying attention (Chapter 6), and nobody cares about your home movies (Chapter 8). They care what the product will do for them.\n10. **If it isn't working after steps 1–9, what did you expect?**\n\n**Why it's useful:** read forward, the steps sound like reasonable 'best practices', and that's exactly why owners follow them. Read backward, the page is a compact audit checklist for any ad. It pairs with the next chapter, the Twelve Most Common Mistakes, which states the same ideas plainly.",
      keyPoints: [
        "The chapter is satire. Every 'step' is a mistake, so do the opposite.",
        "Message beats targeting, and repetition beats reach.",
        "An ad that sounds like an ad, is slick, or full of jargon gets ignored.",
        "Killing ads because friends dislike them, or swapping them after 20–30 runs, throws away the results.",
        "Inverted, the ten steps make a quick audit checklist for any campaign."
      ],
      why: "Almost every local ad ZDub will audit is following several of these steps 'correctly'. Turning the page into a scorecard gives Zach a fast, credible way to show a prospect exactly what's wrong without insulting them.",
