      idea: "Chapter 35 is the plain-language version of Chapter 34's satire, and nearly a summary of Part One. This lesson covers mistakes 1–6. The next covers 7–12.\n\n**1. Wanting instant gratification.** An ad urgent enough to make people respond immediately is the ad most likely to be forgotten the moment the offer expires. It does almost nothing to build the advertiser's identity in people's minds. This is Chapter 20's 'cocaine of advertising' and Chapter 26's warning that urgency ads wear out.\n\n**2. Trying to reach more people than the budget allows.** Each part of a media mix needs enough repetition to be remembered. Spread too thin, you reach lots of people without enough repetition. Roy's framing: you can reach everyone and persuade them a tenth of the way, or reach a tenth of the people and persuade them all the way, **and the cost is the same.** Choose depth.\n\n**3. Assuming the owner knows best.** The owner is *uniquely unqualified* to see his own business objectively. Too much product knowledge leads him to answer questions nobody's asking. He's inside, looking out, trying to describe himself to someone outside looking in. You can't read the label from inside the bottle.\n\n**4. Unsupported claims.** Claiming 'highest quality at the lowest price' without evidence is just a cliché people are tired of. Prove what you say **in every ad.** Ask: does it give the prospect new information, or a new perspective? If not, expect disappointing results (see Chapters 11, 22 and 29).\n\n**5. Misusing passive media.** Non-intrusive media (in 1998, newspapers and the Yellow Pages) reach mostly people who are *already shopping*. They're poor at reaching people before the need arises, so they can't plant a reticular activator (Chapter 16) or build a preference for you in advance. Patient, consistent intrusive media (radio, TV) wins her heart before she's in the market. **Tell her *why* now, and wait for *when*.**\n\n**6. Making ads instead of campaigns.** No single ad can tell your whole story. The most effective ads are like a rhinoceros: **they make one point, powerfully.** If you have seventeen things to say, commit to a campaign of at least seventeen ads, each repeated enough to stick.\n\n**Translated to 2026:** Google Search, Yelp and 'near me' results are passive. They capture *when*. Meta, YouTube and streaming audio are intrusive. They plant *why*. A small budget split across six platforms and ten audiences is mistake #2. A carousel listing nine benefits is mistake #6.",
      keyPoints: [
        "#1 Urgency gets forgotten when the offer expires and builds no identity.",
        "#2 Persuading fewer people all the way costs the same as persuading everyone a little. Choose depth.",
        "#3 The owner is inside the bottle and answers questions nobody asks.",
        "#4 Every ad needs proof: new information or a new perspective.",
        "#5 Intrusive media builds preference before the need ('Why'). Passive media only catches people already shopping ('When').",
        "#6 One ad, one point, like a rhino. Many points means a campaign of many ads."
      ],
      why: "Local businesses run on small budgets, so mistakes #2 and #6 quietly kill most of them: too many channels at too little frequency, and too many messages per ad. ZDub can win simply by concentrating.",
