      idea: "The second half of Roy's list.\n\n**7. Obeying unwritten rules.** For some reason advertisers want their ads to look and sound like ads. Roy only asks: *why?* One word, because he's already made the case in Chapters 25 and 31 (the Non Ad).\n\n**8. Late-week schedules.** Advertisers pile into Thursday and Friday to catch the customer 'right before she shops.' So every competitor is shouting at her on the same two days. Roy asks why you'd fight that crowd when you could have **a quiet chat, alone, on Sunday, Monday and Tuesday.** The principle goes beyond days of the week: put your message where the noise is lowest, and let a predisposition you built earlier do the work at shopping time.\n\n**9. Overconfidence in qualitative targeting.** Advertisers and media reps greatly overrate audience 'quality.' **Far more campaigns die from the wrong message than from the wrong audience.** Say the right thing and a surprising number of people *turn into* the right people. (This is Chapters 14–15 in one line.)\n\n**10. Event-driven marketing.** Judge a special event *only* by whether it helps define your market position and back up your claims. Roy's math: if even **1%** of the people who hear your event ad show up, you'll need a traffic cop and shuttle buses. So your real investment is in the **99% who didn't come.** *What did your ad tell them?* If it only said 'big sale, free hot dogs,' it told them nothing (see Chapter 21's pink gorilla).\n\n**11. Great production without great copy.** Too many ads are creative but don't persuade. Polish, wit, humor and novelty can't replace the four qualities that sell: being **informative, believable, memorable and persuasive.** (Chapter 29 calls this poppin' the rag.)\n\n**12. Confusing reactions with results.** Advertising's goal is clear awareness of your company and its unique selling proposition. But most advertisers grade their ads by the comments they hear from people around them. Mistake response for results and you'll build attention-getting ads that say nothing. (Chapter 23 shows clients killing their best lines because of a spouse or neighbor. Chapter 24 notes complaints often come *with* success.)\n\n**2026 translation:** #9 is why Meta's broad targeting often beats tight interest stacks: the creative does the targeting. #12 is the vanity-metric trap: likes, comments and 'my buddy loved the ad' vs. booked classes, calls and revenue. #10 covers every 'open house,' 'grand opening' and 'customer appreciation day' a local client wants to promote.",
