      idea: "Part Two, *Turning Strangers into Customers*, opens with a salesman. **Charlie Myers** sold used cars from a run-down lot on the wrong side of town, and he sold a lot of them. He knew nothing about cars. He knew a great deal about people.\n\nThree things about Charlie:\n1. **His attitude.** He was instantly and permanently your friend, and it wasn't fake. He believed each day would be the best day of his life, and often it was.\n2. **How he dressed.** The first time Roy met him, Charlie was dressed like a man going to the bank for a loan. Asked why, he said no reason: when you look right, you act right. The key detail is that **he didn't dress up to impress customers. He did it for himself.** It gave him confidence and made him feel in control, or as Charlie put it, *ready.*\n3. **His faith in fit.** Roy pointed at the dullest car on the lot (solid beige, no trim, a bench seat) and asked how anyone would ever sell it. Charlie said there's a butt for every seat. That car was **somebody's dream car**, and he was just waiting for that somebody to arrive.\n\nCharlie was eventually discovered by a radio station and became 'Cheerful Charlie,' one of the most successful morning-drive deejays of the decade. The attitude that sold beige sedans held tens of thousands of listeners every morning.\n\nRoy spells out the point: **selling isn't about having the right inventory. Selling is 'acting right.'** Then he turns it on you. The story isn't really about Charlie. It's about your opportunities (all around you), your inventory (far more exciting than Charlie's) and your customers (ready to buy today). He ends with an order: go sell something.\n\n**What's underneath:**\n- *State before skill.* Charlie managed his own readiness first (clothes, beliefs, expectations), and the selling came out of that.\n- *Don't judge the product by your own taste.* The beige car looked unsellable to Roy because Roy didn't want it. Charlie's question was *who* wants it, not *does anyone*.\n- This sets up the rest of Part Two: Chapter 44 (different temperaments want different things), Chapter 47 (passion sells), Chapter 48 (the excited salesperson closes half) and Chapter 45 (basic, ordinary products make fortunes).",
